contract:
The shared HTTP + SSE contract, verbatim from packages/chess-core/src/api.ts (exported from @chess-tutor/chess-core; coordinator-owned, do not edit; report any gap in contract_gaps):

import type { Color } from "./types.ts";

/**
 * HTTP + SSE contract between apps/server and apps/web (#6: FR-1, FR-4, FR-5, IR-5).
 * Coordinator-owned: change it only on main, before the spokes that depend on it.
 *
 * Endpoints (JSON bodies, all under /api; errors are `ApiError` with the status shown):
 *   POST /api/games                 NewGameRequest -> 201 GameView
 *        If the player is Black, botThinking is true and the bot's first move arrives over SSE.
 *        Creating a game while another is still "playing" marks the old one "abandoned" (and saves it).
 *   GET  /api/games/:id             -> 200 GameView | 404
 *   POST /api/games/:id/moves       PlayMoveRequest -> 200 GameView with the player's move applied
 *        (botThinking true unless the game just ended). 400 illegal or malformed move;
 *        409 not the player's turn, bot thinking, or game not "playing"; 404 unknown game.
 *   POST /api/games/:id/takeback    -> 200 GameView, back at the player's previous turn
 *        (removes the bot's reply, if any, and the player's last move). 409 while the bot is
 *        thinking, when the game is not "playing", or when the player has no move to undo.
 *   POST /api/games/:id/resign      -> 200 GameView, status "finished", endReason "resigned".
 *   GET  /api/games/:id/events      -> text/event-stream. Each message is `event: <GameEvent.type>`
 *        plus `data: <GameEvent as JSON>`. The first message on every connection is "state".
 *
 * Finished and abandoned games are saved as PGN (FR-5). The bot replies within 2 s (NFR-3).
 */

export type GameStatus = "playing" | "finished" | "abandoned";
export type GameResult = "1-0" | "0-1" | "1/2-1/2" | "*";
export type GameEndReason =
  | "checkmate"
  | "stalemate"
  | "threefold_repetition"
  | "insufficient_material"
  | "fifty_moves"
  | "resigned"
  | "abandoned";

export interface MoveView {
  uci: string;
  san: string;
  by: "player" | "bot";
}

export interface GameView {
  id: string;
  playerColor: Color;
  /** FEN of the current position. */
  fen: string;
  /** Every move from the start position, in order. */
  moves: MoveView[];
  status: GameStatus;
  /** "*" while playing or when abandoned. */
  result: GameResult;
  endReason: GameEndReason | null;
  /** True while the server is choosing the bot's reply; the board accepts no player move then. */
  botThinking: boolean;
}

export interface NewGameRequest {
  playerColor: Color;
}

export interface PlayMoveRequest {
  /** UCI, e.g. "e2e4", "e7e8q"; castling as king two squares ("e1g1"). */
  uci: string;
}

export type GameEvent =
  | { type: "state"; game: GameView }
  | { type: "bot_move"; move: MoveView; game: GameView }
  | { type: "game_over"; game: GameView }
  | { type: "error"; message: string };

export interface ApiError {
  error: string;
}

Color is "w" | "b" (packages/chess-core/src/types.ts).

Ports: apps/server listens on PORT (default 3001, constant DEFAULT_PORT in apps/server/src/main.ts placeholder). apps/web/vite.config.ts serves on 5173 and proxies /api to http://localhost:${SERVER_PORT ?? 3001}. Root `pnpm dev` runs `pnpm --parallel --filter "./apps/*" dev`.

E2E arrangement (both spokes must match it): Playwright runs from apps/web. Its webServer starts the real server with env PORT=3101, DB_PATH=:memory:, LICHESS_TOKEN="" (unset/empty => the server's bot plays engine-only, no network), via `pnpm --filter @chess-tutor/server start`, and vite with SERVER_PORT=3101 on port 5174 (--strictPort). The server must honour PORT, DB_PATH and an empty LICHESS_TOKEN exactly like that.

What is on main in apps/web: package.json (deps @chess-tutor/chess-core workspace, chess.js ^1.4.0, react ^19.3.1, react-dom ^19.3.1, react-chessboard 5.12.1; dev deps @playwright/test ^1.65.0, @vitejs/plugin-react, vite ^8.3.6; scripts dev=vite, build=vite build, typecheck=tsc --noEmit, test:e2e=playwright test), index.html, src/main.tsx placeholder, vite.config.ts as above, tsconfig.json including src, e2e, vite.config.ts, playwright.config.ts (lib DOM, jsx react-jsx, moduleResolution Bundler). Root package.json has test:e2e = pnpm --filter @chess-tutor/web test:e2e. react-chessboard 5.x has a different API from 4.x (an options object prop): read node_modules/react-chessboard's types, don't code from memory.

Decisions made by the coordinator:
- The server is the authority. The client uses chess.js on GameView.fen only to decide which drops/clicks are legal and to show legal targets; after a 400 it snaps back to the server's FEN. The board is oriented to playerColor and locked while botThinking or status !== "playing".
- Promotion: auto-queen is acceptable this round; note it in contract_gaps if you don't build a picker.
- Subscribe with EventSource to /api/games/:id/events; on "state", "bot_move", "game_over" replace local GameView with event.game; show "error" events in the status line. Close the EventSource when a new game starts or on unmount.
- Keep the networking and state logic in plain TS modules (an API client and a reducer/store) so it is unit-testable with Vitest without a DOM: there is no jsdom or Testing Library in the repo and you may not add dependencies. Unit tests live under apps/web/test/**/*.test.ts (picked up by the root vitest include apps/*/test/**/*.test.ts). Playwright specs live under apps/web/e2e/ and must not match that vitest include.
- Give the important elements stable data-testid hooks (new-game button, color choice, move list items, takeback, resign, status) and make moves possible by clicking source then target square, so the e2e test does not depend on drag gestures.
- playwright.config.ts: chromium only, the two webServer entries from the E2E arrangement, baseURL http://localhost:5174, reuseExistingServer off in CI (process.env.CI). Allow overriding the server command with env E2E_API_COMMAND (default: the real server command) so you can verify in your worktree before the real server exists: the real server is being built in parallel and is not in your worktree. For your own verification write a throwaway fake API server in your scratch_dir (NOT committed) that implements the contract (e.g. bot plays the first legal move after ~200 ms), and run the e2e against it with E2E_API_COMMAND. The coordinator will run the committed spec against the real server after integrating.
- E2E spec (keep it a few moves, it runs against real Stockfish in CI): as White play d4 and Bf4, wait for each bot reply to appear in the move list, take back once and assert the list shrank by two plies and it is your move; then start a new game as Black and assert the bot's first move appears. Generous timeouts for bot replies (e.g. 10 s), no fixed sleeps.
- Styling: simple, clean, readable; the board is the focus. Light and dark both fine. No CSS framework dependency.

